Brazilian Portuguese speaker learning US English, B1, in a job interview, asking the interviewer to repeat, rephrase or slow down ("Could you repeat that?", "Could you rephrase the question?", "more slowly", "Let me make sure I understood"). Respellings have exactly the word's syllables and never the vowel Brazilians add: "repeat" is "ri-PIT", "rephrase" is "ri-FREIZ", "slowly" is "SLOU-li", "question" is "CUÉS-tchan", never "i-ri-PIT", "a-ri-FREIZ" or "is-LOU-li". Words starting with an English "r" get a tip on making it. B1 sentences fit a real interview, not A1 greetings. The lesson is shared by learners of every field: the learner's own lines never give them a job or field ("my experience in sales"), only general ones ("my most recent role").